"critique":"The primary focal point is completely missing. The center of the screen, which should be dominated by the large 3D Rubik's Cube, is an empty black void. This creates a massive dead area that breaks the reading flow and violates the brief's requirement for a 'large dimensional scrambled cube the centerpiece.' The visual hierarchy is inverted: the secondary controls (Cube Size, Sticker Map, Controls) on the left and right are the only visible elements, causing the user's eye to wander between the side panels rather than focusing on a central object. The 'Sticker Map' panel on the left is overly prominent and dense, competing for attention that should be reserved for the cube itself. The 'Method & Tips' panel on the right is also dense and pushes the 'Game Status' (Timer/Moves) up, but without the cube, the status feels disconnected from the object it is measuring.",
"suggestion":"1. Immediately render the 3D Rubik's Cube in the center of the screen, scaling it to occupy at least 50-60% of the vertical space to establish it as the primary focal point. 2. Reduce the visual weight of the left sidebar (Cube Size and Sticker Map) by using smaller typography and less saturated colors so they recede behind the cube. 3. Group the 'Game Status' (Timer/Moves) directly above or below the cube in the center column to create a clear relationship between the object and its metrics. 4. Move the 'Method & Tips' panel to a collapsible drawer or a lower-priority footer area to reduce visual clutter on the right side, allowing the 'Controls' (Scramble, Undo, etc.) to sit closer to the cube for better tactile proximity."
}

